  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 71-year-old man who is a former smoker with type 2 diabetes and chronic eosinophilia underwent a noncontrast CT chest for lung cancer screening. The CT revealed new tree-in-bud micronodularity in the right lower lobe, along with bronchial wall thickening and mucus plugging, not present on imaging from 5 years prior. He remembers a self-limited cough several weeks ago, has no current respiratory symptoms, and has no other systemic complaints or history of reflux. Is additional follow-up needed?",
    "recommendations": "Leading considerations are atypical infection (eg, Mycobacterium avium complex), allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, aspiration, desquamative interstitial pneumonia, or other bronchiolar processes. We recommend obtaining induced sputum on three consecutive mornings for AFB smear and culture plus fungal and bacterial cultures, and ordering Aspergillus serologies (total IgE, Aspergillus-specific IgE and IgG). Evaluate for aspiration risk (eg, swallow study or review of reflux history). Repeat CT chest in 3 months rather than waiting one year to confirm stability or resolution; if nodularity persists or worsens, or if cough recurs, refer to pulmonology for further assessment (including consideration of bronchoscopy)."
